Dear Make: Editors,

I would like to pitch a reproducible workshop project built around the
released Solidon3D 0.3.3, rather than a general software announcement.

Start with a downloaded bracket whose four mounting holes are in the wrong
place and the wrong size. Solidon recognises them in the mesh. Click one, and
a panel on the right shows what was measured (position, diameter, depth, axis)
as editable fields. Tick "apply to all of the same kind" and the whole
row moves and resizes as one transaction with one undo. Then add a heat-set
insert whose clearance comes from a small stepped gauge printed on the same
machine with the same filament.

The article can answer three practical questions:

1. Does moving a feature preserve its axis and its identity, so that a fit
   defined against it still holds?
2. Does one measured gauge predict the fit of a different part?
3. Does the distance the software reports between two holes match the printed
   result?

The current version is what makes this possible. Recognised features, so holes,
pegs, countersinks and domes, can now be moved, rotated, resized, duplicated
and removed. For a long time only resizing worked; moving is the part that was
missing. The workflow does not require an account, cloud
storage or the optional AI chat.

Overview: https://solidon3d.de/en/

I can supply a complete project kit: calibration and comparison models, a
measurement sheet, step-by-step images, final 3MF files and the released build
for Windows, macOS or Linux. There is no requirement to cover the software
positively, or at all.

